{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-53226",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14249809/assets/458969bfa6aa8d0638f3750d8989770ed7833f0901ae7a8d2c4bfc5ca5004ce9.png)\n\nBy the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠DOA & =m∠BOA-m∠BOD \\\\  & ={140}^{∘}-{80}^{∘} \\\\  & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠DOA=m∠COD={60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOC & =m∠BOD-m∠COD \\\\  & ={80}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={20}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
